                <pb facs="0936" n="856" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg856"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.48A6 Liquor/12: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul at Capetown</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">De
                            Courcy</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-07-22T18:00:00-04:00">July 22, 1936—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your July 20, 3 p.m. Department of Agriculture states that BPQ 362 (May 1, 1934)
                    made provision for entry of grapes from countries where the Mediterranean fruit
                    fly is known to exist if shipments were made in compliance with certain
                    specifications. This method is available to South African shippers as well as to
                    other exporters now sending fruits to the United States. Inasmuch as the South
                    Africans have indicated they could not conveniently use this method experiments
                    were undertaken following exchange of views with the South African entomologists
                    to determine if methods more suitable to their particular needs could be
                    devised. This experimental work is actively in progress but it is difficult to
                    say when results may be expected which would permit a revision of BPQ 362.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
